 "id": "2026-11-28-nvidia-southeast-asia-halt",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "Nvidia reportedly froze A.I. servers bound for four Asian nations. The target is elsewhere."
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "The Financial Times reports Nvidia told partners like Dell and Supermicro to stop shipping servers to Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia, citing three people familiar with it. Nvidia declined to comment. It is unclear if the halt is temporary."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The trigger is a new U.S. rule. Since November nineteenth, advanced A.I. chips for those four countries, and Brazil, need a U.S. license. Washington cites a higher than normal risk of diversion. U.S. based companies are exempt."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "The target is China. Chinese firms can rent computing in data centers abroad. The Wall Street Journal, citing the Institute for Progress, reports such centers supplied about a quarter of China's A.I. computing. Malaysia says its data centers should follow the rule."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will Nvidia servers reach Southeast Asia again this year? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
