 "upload": {
  "title": "Nvidia Reportedly Freezes AI Servers to 4 Asian Nations #shorts",
  "description": "Nvidia has told server partners such as Dell and Supermicro to halt AI server shipments to Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia, the Financial Times reported on November 26, 2026, citing three people familiar with the matter; Nvidia declined to comment and it is unclear whether the halt is temporary. It follows a U.S. Commerce Department rule, in force since November 19, that requires licenses for advanced AI chips sent to those four countries and Brazil, citing a higher-than-normal risk of diversion, with an exemption for U.S.-based companies. The rule targets Chinese firms renting computing power remotely in data centers abroad; the Wall Street Journal, citing the Institute for Progress, reports such data centers supplied about 24% of China's AI computing capacity.\n\nSources: Financial Times (via Reuters, Taipei Times, Manila Times, New Straits Times, Gizmodo, TechRepublic, Tech Times), U.S. Commerce Department BIS rule (via TechCrunch, Latham & Watkins, The Diplomat), Wall Street Journal / Institute for Progress, The Star (Malaysia).\n\n#tech #Nvidia #AI #chips #exportcontrols #SoutheastAsia",
